 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_4YfHY6sGGvMBwfvz",
   "name": "Laws of Logarithms",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "819054451"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Laws of logarithms"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are the laws of logarithms?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Laws of logarithms allow you to simplify and manipulate expressions involving logarithms\n\n  - The laws of logarithms are equivalent to the **laws of indices**\n- The laws you need to know are, given $a,x,y>0$:\n\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}xy=\\mathrm{log}_{a}x+\\mathrm{log}_{a}y$\n  \n    - This relates to $a^{x}\\timesa^{y}=a^{x+y}$\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}\\frac{x}{y}=\\mathrm{log}_{a}x-\\mathrm{log}_{a}y$\n  \n    - This relates to $a^{x}\\diva^{y}=a^{x-y}$\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}x^{m}=m\\mathrm{log}_{a}x$\n  \n    - This relates to $(a^{x})^{y}=a^{xy}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/6.2.1-Laws-of-Logarithms-Notes-fig2.png",
   "file": "assets/b3870da6fd3f-6-2-1-laws-of-logarithms-notes-fig2.png",
   "alt": "The laws of logarithms",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- There are also some particular results these lead to\n\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}a=1$\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}a^{x}=x$\n  - $a^{\\mathrm{log}_{a}x}=x$\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}1=0$\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\mathrm{log}_{a}x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/6.2.1-Laws-of-Logarithms-Notes-fig3.png",
   "file": "assets/c9b61f5fe62a-6-2-1-laws-of-logarithms-notes-fig3.png",
   "alt": "Properties of logarithms",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Beware…\n\n  - …$log_{a} ( x + y ) \\text{eq} log_{a} x + log_{a} y$\n- These results apply to $\\mathrm{ln}x(\\mathrm{log}_{e}x)$ too\n\n  - Two particularly useful results are\n  \n    - $\\mathrm{ln}e^{x}=x$\n    - $e^{\\mathrm{ln}x}=x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use the laws of logarithms?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Laws of logarithms can be used to …\n\n  - … simplify expressions\n  - … solve logarithmic equations\n  - … solve exponential equations"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/6.2.1-Laws-of-Logarithms-Notes-fig4.png",
   "file": "assets/e87d814c47bc-6-2-1-laws-of-logarithms-notes-fig4.png",
   "alt": "Simplifying an expression using the laws of logarithms",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Remember to check whether your solutions are valid\n\n  - log (x+k) is only defined if x > -k\n  - You will lose marks if you forget to reject invalid solutions"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "a) Write the expression $2\\mathrm{log}4-\\mathrm{log}2$ in the form $\\mathrm{log}k$, where $k\\inℤ$.\n\n> *Using the law* $\\mathrm{log}_{a}x^{m}=m\\mathrm{log}_{a}x$ *we can rewrite* $2\\mathrm{log}4$ *as* $\\mathrm{log}4^{2}=\\mathrm{log}16$*.*\n\n> *  *\n\n$2\\mathrm{log}4-\\mathrm{log}2=\\mathrm{log}16-\\mathrm{log}2$\n\n> *Using the law* $\\mathrm{log}_{a}\\frac{x}{y}=\\mathrm{log}_{a}x-\\mathrm{log}_{a}y$*:*\n\n> * *\n\n> * *$\\mathrm{log}16-\\mathrm{log}2=\\mathrm{log}\\frac{16}{2}=\\mathrm{log}8$\n\n$2\\mathrm{log}4-\\mathrm{log}2=\\mathrm{log}8$\n\nb)   Hence, or otherwise, solve $2\\mathrm{log}4-\\mathrm{log}2=-\\mathrm{log}\\frac{1}{x}$.\n\n> *Rewrite the equation using the expression found in part (a).*\n\n> $2\\mathrm{log}4-\\mathrm{log}2=\\mathrm{log}8$*:*\n\n> *  *\n\n$\\mathrm{log}8=-\\mathrm{log}\\frac{1}{x}$\n\n> *Using the index law* $\\frac{1}{x}=x^{-1}$*::*\n\n> * *\n\n> * *$\\mathrm{log}8=-\\mathrm{log}x^{-1}$\n\n> *Using the law* $\\mathrm{log}_{a}x^{m}=m\\mathrm{log}_{a}x$*:*\n\n> *  *\n\n$\\log 8 = \\log x^{- ( - 1 )} = \\log x$\n\n> * *\n\n> *Compare the two sides.*\n\n> * *$\\mathrm{log}8=\\mathrm{log}x$\n\n$x=8$"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_7D6JfdxSZVhhGkYM",
   "name": "Change of Base",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Change of base"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I change the base of a logarithm?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The formula for changing the base of a logarithm is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\mathrm{log}_{a}x=\\frac{\\mathrm{log}_{b}x}{\\mathrm{log}_{b}a}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The value you choose for *b* does not matter, however if you do not have a calculator, you can choose *b* such that the problem will be possible to solve"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Why change the base of a logarithm?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The laws of logarithms can only be used if the logs have the same** base**\n\n  - If a problem involves logarithms with different bases, you can change the base of the logarithm and then apply the laws of logarithms\n- **Changing the base **of a logarithm can be particularly useful if you need to evaluate a log problem **without a calculator**\n\n  - Choose the base such that you would know how to solve the problem from the equivalent exponent"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This formula had more use when calculators were less advanced\n\n  - Some old calculators only had a button for logarithm of base 10\n  - To calculate $\\mathrm{log}_{5}7$on these calculators you would have to enter\n  \n    - $\\frac{log_{10} 7}{log_{10} 5}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The formula can be useful when evaluating a logarithm where the two numbers are **powers of a common number**\n\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{4}8=\\frac{\\mathrm{log}_{2}8}{\\mathrm{log}_{2}4}=\\frac{3}{2}$\n- The formula can be useful when you are solving equations and two logarithms have **different bases**\n\n  - For example, if you have $\\mathrm{log}_{3}k$ and $\\mathrm{log}_{9}n$ within the same equation\n  \n    - You can rewrite $\\mathrm{log}_{9}n$ as $\\frac{log_{3} n}{log_{3} 9}$ which simplifies to $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{log}_{3}n$\n    - Or you can rewrite $\\mathrm{log}_{3}k$ as $\\frac{log_{9} k}{log_{9} 3}$ which simplifies to $2\\mathrm{log}_{9}k$\n- The formula also allows you to derive and use a formula for switching the numbers:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$log_{a} x = \\frac{1}{log_{x} a}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Using the fact that $\\mathrm{log}_{x}x=1$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- It is very rare that you will need to use the change of base formula\n- Only use it when the bases of the logarithms are different"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "By choosing a suitable value for *b,* use the change of base law to find the value of  $\\mathrm{log}_{8}32$ without using a calculator.\n\n> *Note that 8 and 32 are both powers of 2, where 8 = 2*<sup>*3* </sup>*and 32 = 2*<sup>*5*</sup>*.*\n\n> *Therefore we can choose **b** = 2 in the change of base formula. *\n\n> $\\mathrm{log}_{a}x=\\frac{\\mathrm{log}_{b}x}{\\mathrm{log}_{b}a}$*:*\n\n> *  *\n\n$\\mathrm{log}_{8}32=\\frac{\\mathrm{log}_{2}32}{\\mathrm{log}_{2}8}$\n\n> *If  2*<sup>*3*</sup>*= 8 then log*<sub>*2* </sub>*8 = 3 and if  2*<sup>*5*</sup>*= 32 then log*<sub>*2* </sub>*32 = 5.*\n\n> * *\n\n> * *$\\frac{\\mathrm{log}_{2}32}{\\mathrm{log}_{2}8}=\\frac{5}{3}$\n\n$\\mathrm{log}_{8}32=\\frac{5}{3}=1\\frac{2}{3}$"
  }
 ]
}